Consider a quantitative human phenotype Y measured in a cohort of N individuals, each genotyped at a set of genetic variants partitioned by minor allele frequency (MAF) into common variants (MAF > 1%) and rare variants (MAF ≤ 1%, including ultra-rare singletons). The narrow-sense heritability h² of Y is defined as the proportion of phenotypic variance attributable to additive genetic effects across all variants in the genome. Empirical genome-wide association studies typically recover only a fraction of h² from common variants (the "missing heritability" gap), and rare variants are hypothesized to contribute disproportionately large per-allele effects but are difficult to detect because (a) their low frequency yields low statistical power per variant, (b) capturing them requires whole-genome or whole-exome sequencing rather than genotyping arrays, creating a trade-off between sequencing depth/quality and achievable cohort size N, and (c) standard single-variant association tests and additive polygenic risk score (PRS) constructions become statistically unreliable when applied to variants with very low allele counts.

Develop a principled statistical methodology that:
(i) defines an estimator of the contribution of rare variants to h² which is consistent and has quantifiable bias and variance as a function of cohort size N, sequencing error model, and MAF spectrum;
(ii) specifies, under a fixed total sequencing budget B, the optimal trade-off between per-sample sequencing coverage (quality) and N (quantity) for maximizing power to detect rare-variant associations and for minimizing mean-squared prediction error of a polygenic risk score that incorporates rare variants;
(iii) gives a rigorous interpretive framework — for example, a variant-aggregation or burden-test scheme on biologically defined units (genes, regulatory elements) — under which rare-variant association signals can be assigned effect sizes and incorporated into a PRS in a way that improves out-of-sample predictive accuracy over common-variant-only PRS.

Prove or disprove that, under reasonable generative models of complex-trait architecture (e.g., a mixture of common small-effect and rare large-effect causal variants), such a methodology can close a quantifiable portion of the missing-heritability gap, and characterize the regimes of (N, coverage, MAF distribution, effect-size distribution) in which it does so.

